<TopicPage slug="subjects/phlebotomy/foundational/veins-and-blood-the-phlebotomist-s-map">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phlebotomy is the procedure that starts most diagnoses: the blood draw. It looks simple and is
    not — it is anatomy (which vein), chemistry (which tube, which order) and trust (a needle, a
    stranger, a steady hand) compressed into five minutes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from patient ID to bedside labelling" widget="equationStep" config={orderCfg} />

  <h2>Why the order of draw is chemistry law</h2>
  <p>
    Each tube's additive does a different job — citrate anticoagulates clot tests, EDTA preserves
    cell counts, fluoride preserves glucose. The needle carries traces of each additive to the next
    tube, so drawing tubes out of order cross-contaminates them: EDTA ruins potassium results,
    citrate ruins clotting tests. The sequence is not bureaucratic; it is protection against
    invisible chemistry.
  </p>

  <h2>Site selection is anatomy applied</h2>
  <p>
    The median cubital vein wins because it is usually large, superficial and — critically —
    sits away from the median nerve and brachial artery, the two structures a careless needle can
    find. Basilic draws carry higher nerve-injury risk. Anatomy here is not academic: it is the
    difference between a routine draw and an injury claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Trust is part of the technique</h2>
  <p>
    Patients faint, flinch and fear needles — the phlebotomist's training includes positioning for
    fainting, honest distraction, and the discipline to stop after two failed sticks and call a
    colleague. The profession's measure is a confident draw the patient barely noticed.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASCP BOC PBT content outline; CLSI GP41 venipuncture standard; Ernst, <em>Applied Phlebotomy</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
